+    // Multicast video arrives in bursts (especially on scene changes / IDR frames), and in
+    // this player the H.264/H.265 decode runs synchronously on the live555 event-loop
+    // thread (see StreamSink::afterGettingFrame -> videoCallback). While a frame is being
+    // decoded nobody is reading the socket, so the kernel receive buffer is the only thing
+    // that absorbs the burst. live555's MultiFramedRTPSource only asks for 50 KB, which
+    // overflows on a scene change and drops the RTP packets that carry the middle of a
+    // frame; the resulting gap in the MPEG-TS / H.264 byte stream is exactly what produces
+    // the "cbp too large" / "out of range intra chroma pred mode" / "concealing N errors"
+    // decoder messages and the on-screen tearing. VLC does not show this because it drains
+    // the socket on a dedicated thread and uses a much larger buffer. Ask for a generous
+    // buffer here so the burst fits even while the event loop is briefly busy decoding.
+    // increaseReceiveBufferTo() only ever grows the buffer, so the 50 KB request made later
+    // inside MultiFramedRTPSource's constructor becomes a no-op.
+    {
+        unsigned granted = increaseReceiveBufferTo(*environment, rtpSocket.socketNum(), 2 * 1024 * 1024);
+        *environment << "RTP socket receive buffer: " << granted << " bytes\n";
+    }
